han assumed).\n  V3: summed distance with normalization disabled (raw character edit distance on the final answer string).\n  V4: summed distance with stronger normalization (case-fold, strip punctuation/whitespace, canonical answer extraction).\nInterpretation rule: the conclusion \"MinED outperforms EM\" is reported as robust if ALL variants agree in direction; if variants disagree, the result is reported as formulation-sensitive with per-variant deltas shown. This converts U1/U2-style ambiguity into an explicit analysis output instead of a blocker.\n\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\n7. METRICS AND ANALYSIS PLAN\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\nPrimary endpoint (pre-registered): overall BBH exact-match accuracy (micro over all 6,511 questions, all 27 tasks), MinED arm vs. EM arm, N = 8.\nSecondary endpoints (pre-registered): (a) free-form subset (4 tasks) accuracy \u2014 the expected locus of any effect per A3; (b) MC subset (23 tasks) accuracy \u2014 expected \u2248 0 delta, serving as a built-in negative control; (c) per-task accuracies (27 values) for heterogeneity analysis.\nSupporting metrics: arm agreement rate (fraction of questions where arms pick the same candidate); pass@N ceiling; greedy baseline; per-task selection-margin distributions (e.g., MinED winner's distance gap).\n\nStatistical tests:\n  1. Paired per-question test on the full benchmark: McNemar's test on the discordant-pair counts (MinED-only correct vs. EM-only correct), aggregated across all questions.\n  2. Cluster-aware inference: questions are nested in tasks, so also run a cluster bootstrap (resample tasks with replacement, B = 10,000) on the accuracy delta; report bootstrap 95% CI for \u0394 = acc(MinED) \u2212 acc(EM). A seed-level check (3 seeds) supplements this: report per-seed deltas and their sign consistency.\n  3. Stratified analyses: McNemar + cluster bootstrap separately on the free-form subset and the MC subset.\n  4. Heterogeneity: per-task \u0394 with 95% CIs (bootstrap within task), displayed as a forest plot; task-level random-effects summary as a robustness check on the micro aggregate.\n  5. Multiplicity: the two pre-registered endpoints are reported with no p-value correction (both are confirmatory); all other per-task/per-variant tests are labeled exploratory.\nDecision rule (operationalizing \"outperforms\", per the approved sketch): MinED is declared to outperform EM if (i) \u0394 > 0 on the primary endpoint with McNemar p < 0.05 and a cluster-bootstrap CI excluding 0, AND (ii) direction is consistent across all MinED variants (Section 6) and across seeds. If (i) holds only on the free-form subset, report a qualified finding: \"MinED outperforms EM on free-form BBH tasks, with \u2248parity on MC tasks\" \u2014 which is the mechanistically expected outcome under A3 and still answers the question truthfully.\n\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\n8. FEASIBILITY\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\n- This environment is design-only by construction: /workspace contains only task.json; network is disabled; no GPU, datasets, or ML libraries are installed. No execution is required or attempted, consistent with the task contract.\n- If executed elsewhere, the design is standard and feasible: BBH is public (~6.5k questions); the pipeline is (i) sampling, (ii) two pure-Python selection functions (Levenshtein via a standard DP implementation over \u226416 strings of short answer length; pairwise cost \u2248 N\u00b2/2 distances per question \u2014 trivial CPU cost), (iii) one scoring script, (iv) McNemar + bootstrap analysis in scipy/numpy. The only substantial cost is candidate generation (~156k samples at N = 8 \u00d7 3 seeds), which is a single-model inference job and can be reduced by using the N = 4 primary configuration if compute-limited (the N ablation already covers this).\n- Feasibility risks and mitigations: degenerate candidate pools (all candidates identical \u2192 arms identical by construction; these questions contribute zero discordant pairs and are counted in the denominator); free-form answer-extraction errors (mitigated by the calibration control in Section 4); MC-task degeneracy of MinED (handled by stratified endpoints rather than excluded, so the primary endpoint remains over the full benchmark as fixed by the task context).\n\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\n9. WHAT WOULD VALIDATE OR FALSIFY THE DESIGN\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\n- Falsification of the hypothesis: \u0394 \u2264 0 or CI includes 0 on the primary endpoint \u2192 MinED does not outperform EM on BBH (report as negative/null).\n- Design self-check: the MC subset should show near-zero \u0394; a large positive/negative MC delta would indicate a scoring or tie-breaking artifact rather than a genuine criterion effect, triggering a pipeline audit before interpretation.\n- The pass@N ceiling contextualizes the practical size of any win: \u0394 is more meaningful when pass@N \u2212 EM is large (headroom exists).\n\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\n10. RESIDUAL UNCERTAINTIES (explicitly non-blocking; documented as assumptions)\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\n- Exact EM-selection formulation in the source paper (mode selection assumed under A1; alternatives such as \"select candidate matching the greedy output\" would be a one-line change to the arm definition).\n- MinED formulation (handled by Section 6 sensitivity analysis).\n- Model identity, temperature, and prompt wording (within-model paired design makes conclusions conditional on these choices; a second model family is listed as an optional robustness extension, not a requirement).\n- Free-form scoring variant (exact match vs. contains) \u2014 fixed to standard BBH scoring in the design; noted as assumption.\n- Whether the paper's own numbers should be mirrored \u2014 the design specifies a de novo evaluation; if the researcher later wants replication, only Section 3.2 parameters change.\n\nThis is the complete deliverable: design, rationale, controls, analysis plan, and feasibility. Nothing has been run and no results are claimed.", "evidence": "Repository observations: /workspace contains only task.json (question + design-only note \"repositories and compute are not required\"); filesystem and pip inspection confirm no code, datasets, or ML libraries; network disabled and no GPU, confirming a design-only deliverable. Source setup details come solely from the researcher-approved context: EM (setup_10) and MinED (setup_11) as the two alignment criteria compared; BBH performance (setup_4) as the outcome; full benchmark of 23 MC + 4 free-form tasks (setup_14) with task categories (setup_15). Design choices carried forward from the approved plan: paired within-question shared-pool comparison, stratified reporting (overall primary endpoint, free-form secondary), McNemar + cluster-aware bootstrap statistics, N ablation {4,8,16}, deterministic tie-breaking, pass@N upper-bound reference. New per researcher instruction: assumption A1 declared explicitly (EM = selection criterion, exact-match-to-gold = common final metric) rather than treated as a blocker, and a pre-registered sensitivity analysis over MinED variants (summed pairwise vs. medoid, with/without normalization) so the conclusion does not hinge on one unstated formulation. Scale estimate for feasibility: ~6,511 BBH questions \u00d7 8 samples \u00d7 3 seeds \u2248 156k generations for one model family.", "stage": "result", "assumptions": ["A1 (declared, load-bearing): EM is the alignment/selection criterion (mode/majority selection over exact-matched candidates) compared against MinED; exact-match-to-gold is the common final accuracy metric in both arms", "A2: MinED minimizes an edit-distance objective over the candidate pool; covered by a 4-variant sensitivity analysis instead of one fixed formulation", "A3: On the 23 MC tasks MinED \u2248 mode selection (short candidates), so the discriminating regime is the 4 free-form tasks; free-form subset is a pre-registered secondary endpoint and MC subset acts as a built-in negative control", "A4: One model family, temperature > 0, 3-shot CoT BBH prompts, identical across arms; conclusions are conditional on this choice", "A5: Free-form scoring follows standard BBH answer extraction/normalization"], "uncertainties": ["Exact EM-selection formulation in the source paper (mode selection assumed; alternatives are a one-line arm change)", "MinED's precise formulation (resolved analytically via the pre-registered variant sensitivity analysis)", "Model identity, temperature, and prompt wording are free design choices; a second model family is an optional robustness extension", "Free-form scoring variant (exact vs. contains) is fixed to standard BBH scoring but noted as an assumption", "Whether the deliverable should mirror the source paper's parameters \u2014 design specifies a de novo evaluation, with parameter substitution available if replication is requested"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-2f94ddcb63f94dceb9e9e54e0625a7d3", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "end_researcher_turn: {"answer": "FINAL EXPERIMENT DESIGN (design-only; no execution)\n\nTitle: Does MinED alignment outperform EM alignment on BBH?\n\nRQ: When used as the candidate-selection (alignment) criterion over N sampled outputs per question, does MinED (Minimum Edit Distance) selection yield higher exact-match accuracy on BBH than EM (Exact Matching) selection?\n\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\n1. DECLARED FIXED ELEMENTS (from task context)\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\n- Two arms compared: EM (Exact Matching) alignment criterion vs. MinED (Minimum Edit Distance) alignment criterion.\n- Evaluation benchmark: BBH, full benchmark \u2014 23 multiple-choice (MC) tasks + 4 free-form generation tasks (27 total), spanning the BBH task categories (setup_15 category list to be applied as printed in the source table).\n- Deliverable is a design only; no experiment is run and no results are claimed.\n- Final scoring metric: exact-match-to-gold accuracy, identical in both arms.\n\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\n2. DECLARED DESIGN ASSUMPTIONS (assumptions are stated, not verified \u2014 no source topics resolve them)\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\n- A1 (declared, load-bearing): EM serves as the alignment/selection criterion being compared against MinED \u2014 i.e., EM arm selects among the sampled candidates using exact-matching-based consensus (majority/mode selection: pick the candidate string that exactly matches the largest number of other candidates) \u2014 while exact-match-to-gold is the common final accuracy metric in BOTH arms. Without A1 the question is ill-posed (if EM were only the metric, MinED could not \"outperform\" it as a criterion).\n- A2: MinED selects the candidate minimizing an edit-distance objective over the candidate pool; the exact formulation is uncertain, so the design includes a sensitivity analysis over MinED variants (Section 6) rather than treating this as a blocker.\n- A3: On MC tasks, candidates are short (answer letters), so MinED \u2248 mode selection; the discriminating power of the experiment concentrates on the 4 free-form tasks. The design therefore pre-registers the free-form subset as a secondary endpoint (Section 7).\n- A4: One open-source/instruction-tuned model family is used for all arms (exact model is a free design choice; the comparison is within-model, so results do not hinge on model identity). Sampling uses temperature T > 0 (e.g., T = 0.7) to produce a non-degenerate candidate pool.\n- A5: Chain-of-thought prompting per standard BBH protocol (3-shot CoT prompts from the official BBH repo), identical across arms.\n\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\n3. EXPERIMENTAL DESIGN\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\n3.1 Structure: within-question paired comparison.\nFor every question q in all 27 BBH tasks:\n  Step 1 \u2014 Sample N candidates: y_1..y_N ~ model(\u00b7|prompt_q), fixed seed set S, temperature T, identical prompt.\n  Step 2 \u2014 The SAME candidate pool is passed to both selection arms (shared-pool control; no resampling between arms, so differences reflect the criterion, not sampling variance).\n  Step 3 \u2014 Arm EM: select \u0177_EM(q) = the candidate matching the largest number of co-sampled candidates under exact string match (mode). Ties broken deterministically (Section 5).\n  Step 4 \u2014 Arm MinED: select \u0177_MinED(q) = argmin_i \u03a3_j d(y_i, y_j), d = normalized Levenshtein on lightly normalized outputs (lowercase, strip whitespace/punctuation, extract final answer line). \n  Step 5 \u2014 Score both selections with the same EM-to-gold metric: correct(\u0177, gold) via standard BBH scoring (exact letter match for MC; exact/regex match with the official BBH answer extraction for free-form).\nReference lines: pass@N(q) \u2014 the pool contains at least one gold-matching candidate \u2014 computed on the same pools as a ceiling reference; single-sample greedy baseline (N=1) as a floor reference.\n\n3.2 Sample-size configuration:\n- Primary configuration: N = 8 candidates per question, one seed set.\n- N ablation: N \u2208 {4, 8, 16} on a fixed subsample (or full set if compute allows) to test robustness of the MinED\u2212EM delta to pool size.\n- Repeated seeds: 3 independent seed sets at N = 8 to estimate sampling variance and support seed-level inference.\n- Expected scale: BBH has 6,511 questions (4,723 MC + 1,788 free-form). Total generations \u2248 6,511 \u00d7 8 \u00d7 3 seeds \u2248 156k samples \u2014 feasible for one model family; the design does not require execution here.\n\n3.3 Arms and conditions summary:\n  \u2022 E1 (primary): EM selection, N = 8, all 27 tasks.\n  \u2022 E2 (primary): MinED selection, N = 8, all 27 tasks (same pools as E1).\n  \u2022 E3 (sensitivity): MinED variants (Section 6), same pools.\n  \u2022 E4 (ablation): N \u2208 {4, 16}, both arms, same pools per N.\n  \u2022 References: pass@N, greedy N=1 baseline.\n  \u2022 Determinism check: greedy single-sample vs. N=1 sampling sanity check.\n\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\n4. CONTROLS\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\n- Shared candidate pool (identical samples fed to both arms) \u2014 the core control isolating the criterion effect.\n- Identical prompts, decoding parameters, model checkpoint, and scoring pipeline across arms.\n- Fixed, pre-registered tie-breaking (Section 5) applied identically in both arms.\n- Deterministic scoring: single scoring script with a fixed answer-extraction/normalization function, applied post-selection; extraction errors are logged, not hand-corrected post hoc.\n- Scoring pipeline validated on a small held-out hand-labeled subset (e.g., 50 questions/task) before full scoring \u2014 a calibration control, run only if the design were executed.\n- Blinding: the analysis script computes both arms' scores without the analyst inspecting intermediate per-task results before freezing the analysis code.\n\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\n5. TIE-BREAKING AND DETERMINISM RULES (pre-registered)\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\n- EM arm ties among tied-frequency candidates: (i) earliest occurrence index in the pool; if still tied, (ii) lexicographically smallest string.\n- MinED arm ties among tied total distance: same two-stage rule (earliest index, then lexicographic).\n- Duplicate clusters: collapsed before distance computation (duplicates contribute identical distance values; selection over distinct strings, index of first occurrence kept).\n- MC tasks: edit distance computed on the extracted answer letter/token, not the full CoT trace; EM/mode likewise on the extracted token.\n- All random seeds, tie-break rules, and normalization functions fixed in the pre-registration before any (hypothetical) run.\n\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\n6. SENSITIVITY ANALYSIS OVER MinED VARIANTS (per researcher refinement)\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\nBecause the precise MinED formulation is unstated in the available context, the design does not hinge on one variant. Four pre-registered variants, all on the same pools:\n  V1: summed pairwise distance \u2014 argmin_i \u03a3_j d(y_i, y_j).\n  V2: medoid (equivalent to V1 by symmetry of Levenshtein \u2014 reported to confirm equivalence rather than assumed).\n  V3: summed distance with normalization disabled (raw character edit distance on the final answer string).\n  V4: summed distance with stronger normalization (case-fold, strip punctuation/whitespace, canonical answer extraction).\nInterpretation rule: the conclusion \"MinED outperforms EM\" is reported as robust if ALL variants agree in direction; if variants disagree, the result is reported as formulation-sensitive with per-variant deltas shown. This converts U1/U2-style ambiguity into an explicit analysis output instead of a blocker.\n\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\n7. METRICS AND ANALYSIS PLAN\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\nPrimary endpoint (pre-registered): overall BBH exact-match accuracy (micro over all 6,511 questions, all 27 tasks), MinED arm vs. EM arm, N = 8.\nSecondary endpoints (pre-registered): (a) free-form subset (4 tasks) accuracy \u2014 the expected locus of any effect per A3; (b) MC subset (23 tasks) accuracy \u2014 expected \u2248 0 delta, serving as a built-in negative control; (c) per-task accuracies (27 values) for heterogeneity analysis.\nSupporting metrics: arm agreement rate (fraction of questions where arms pick the same candidate); pass@N ceiling; greedy baseline; per-task selection-margin distributions (e.g., MinED winner's distance gap).\n\nStatistical tests:\n  1. Paired per-question test on the full benchmark: McNemar's test on the discordant-pair counts (MinED-only correct vs. EM-only correct), aggregated across all questions.\n  2. Cluster-aware inference: questions are nested in tasks, so also run a cluster bootstrap (resample tasks with replacement, B = 10,000) on the accuracy delta; report bootstrap 95% CI for \u0394 = acc(MinED) \u2212 acc(EM). A seed-level check (3 seeds) supplements this: report per-seed deltas and their sign consistency.\n  3. Stratified analyses: McNemar + cluster bootstrap separately on the free-form subset and the MC subset.\n  4. Heterogeneity: per-task \u0394 with 95% CIs (bootstrap within task), displayed as a forest plot; task-level random-effects summary as a robustness check on the micro aggregate.\n  5. Multiplicity: the two pre-registered endpoints are reported with no p-value correction (both are confirmatory); all other per-task/per-variant tests are labeled exploratory.\nDecision rule (operationalizing \"outperforms\", per the approved sketch): MinED is declared to outperform EM if (i) \u0394 > 0 on the primary endpoint with McNemar p < 0.05 and a cluster-bootstrap CI excluding 0, AND (ii) direction is consistent across all MinED variants (Section 6) and across seeds. If (i) holds only on the free-form subset, report a qualified finding: \"MinED outperforms EM on free-form BBH tasks, with \u2248parity on MC tasks\" \u2014 which is the mechanistically expected outcome under A3 and still answers the question truthfully.\n\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\n8. FEASIBILITY\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\n- This environment is design-only by construction: /workspace contains only task.json; network is disabled; no GPU, datasets, or ML libraries are installed. No execution is required or attempted, consistent with the task contract.\n- If executed elsewhere, the design is standard and feasible: BBH is public (~6.5k questions); the pipeline is (i) sampling, (ii) two pure-Python selection functions (Levenshtein via a standard DP implementation over \u226416 strings of short answer length; pairwise cost \u2248 N\u00b2/2 distances per question \u2014 trivial CPU cost), (iii) one scoring script, (iv) McNemar + bootstrap analysis in scipy/numpy. The only substantial cost is candidate generation (~156k samples at N = 8 \u00d7 3 seeds), which is a single-model inference job and can be reduced by using the N = 4 primary configuration if compute-limited (the N ablation already covers this).\n- Feasibility risks and mitigations: degenerate candidate pools (all candidates identical \u2192 arms identical by construction; these questions contribute zero discordant pairs and are counted in the denominator); free-form answer-extraction errors (mitigated by the calibration control in Section 4); MC-task degeneracy of MinED (handled by stratified endpoints rather than excluded, so the primary endpoint remains over the full benchmark as fixed by the task context).\n\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\n9. WHAT WOULD VALIDATE OR FALSIFY THE DESIGN\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\n- Falsification of the hypothesis: \u0394 \u2264 0 or CI includes 0 on the primary endpoint \u2192 MinED does not outperform EM on BBH (report as negative/null).\n- Design self-check: the MC subset should show near-zero \u0394; a large positive/negative MC delta would indicate a scoring or tie-breaking artifact rather than a genuine criterion effect, triggering a pipeline audit before interpretation.\n- The pass@N ceiling contextualizes the practical size of any win: \u0394 is more meaningful when pass@N \u2212 EM is large (headroom exists).\n\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\n10. RESIDUAL UNCERTAINTIES (explicitly non-blocking; documented as assumptions)\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\n- Exact EM-selection formulation in the source paper (mode selection assumed under A1; alternatives such as \"select candidate matching the greedy output\" would be a one-line change to the arm definition).\n- MinED formulation (handled by Section 6 sensitivity analysis).\n- Model identity, temperature, and prompt wording (within-model paired design makes conclusions conditional on these choices; a second model family is listed as an optional robustness extension, not a requirement).\n- Free-form scoring variant (exact match vs. contains) \u2014 fixed to standard BBH scoring in the design; noted as assumption.\n- Whether the paper's own numbers should be mirrored \u2014 the design specifies a de novo evaluation; if the researcher later wants replication, only Section 3.2 parameters change.\n\nThis is the complete deliverable: design, rationale, controls, analysis plan, and feasibility. Nothing has been run and no results are claimed.", "evidence": "Repository observations: /workspace contains only task.json (question + design-only note \"repositories and compute are not required\"); filesystem and pip inspection confirm no code, datasets, or ML libraries; network disabled and no GPU, confirming a design-only deliverable. Source setup details come solely from the researcher-approved context: EM (setup_10) and MinED (setup_11) as the two alignment criteria compared; BBH performance (setup_4) as the outcome; full benchmark of 23 MC + 4 free-form tasks (setup_14) with task categories (setup_15). Design choices carried forward from the approved plan: paired within-question shared-pool comparison, stratified reporting (overall primary endpoint, free-form secondary), McNemar + cluster-aware bootstrap statistics, N ablation {4,8,16}, deterministic tie-breaking, pass@N upper-bound reference. New per researcher instruction: assumption A1 declared explicitly (EM = selection criterion, exact-match-to-gold = common final metric) rather than treated as a blocker, and a pre-registered sensitivity analysis over MinED variants (summed pairwise vs. medoid, with/without normalization) so the conclusion does not hinge on one unstated formulation. Scale estimate for feasibility: ~6,511 BBH questions \u00d7 8 samples \u00d7 3 seeds \u2248 156k generations for one model family.", "stage": "result", "assumptions": ["A1 (declared, load-bearing): EM is the alignment/selection criterion (mode/majority selection over exact-matched candidates) compared against MinED; exact-match-to-gold is the common final accuracy metric in both arms", "A2: MinED minimizes an edit-distance objective over the candidate pool; covered by a 4-variant sensitivity analysis instead of one fixed formulation", "A3: On the 23 MC tasks MinED \u2248 mode selection (short candidates), so the discriminating regime is the 4 free-form tasks; free-form subset is a pre-registered secondary endpoint and MC subset acts as a built-in negative control", "A4: One model family, temperature > 0, 3-shot CoT BBH prompts, identical across arms; conclusions are conditional on this choice", "A5: Free-form scoring follows standard BBH answer extraction/normalization"], "uncertainties": ["Exact EM-selection formulation in the source paper (mode selection assumed; alternatives are a one-line arm change)", "MinED's precise formulation (resolved analytically via the pre-registered variant sensitivity analysis)", "Model identity, temperature, and prompt wording are free design choices; a second model family is an optional robustness extension", "Free-form scoring variant (exact vs. contains) is fixed to standard BBH scoring but noted as an assumption", "Whether the deliverable should mirror the source paper's parameters \u2014 design specifies a de novo evaluation, with parameter substitution available if replication is requested"]}", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
